## a r t i c l e i n f o
Article history:  Received 5 September 2017  Revised 10 November 2017  Accepted 10 November 2017  Available online 11 November 2017
Keywords:  Metal-organic framework  Fluorescence enhancement  Fluorescence quenching  Cadmium ion  Nitrobenzene
## a b s t r a c t
In this study, a novel three-dimensional zinc-based metal-organic framework (Zn-MOF), i.e., \(\left(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{\mathrm{n}}\) \(\mathrm{L}=2,5\)-bis(phenylamino)-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) was designed and developed under solvothermal condition. As a proof-of-principle, a \(\pi\)-conjugated framework of carboxylate ligand capable of "bottom up" synthesis was integrated with metal ion to construct a novel MOF for sensing applications. As expected, the synthesized Zn-MOF exhibited fluorescence enhancement for cadmium ion \(\left(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\right)\) and sensing of nitrobenzene (NB) through fluorescence quenching. The detection limits were calculated to be \(0.12\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) for \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) and \(1.19\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) for NB based on signal-to-noise ratio of 3:1. Moreover, various techniques and density functional theory investigations verified that the possible sensing mechanisms for \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) and NB included ion exchange and photoinduced electron transfer, respectively. Finally, their practical applications on real samples also demonstrated that the Zn-MOF-based sensor can be effectively utilized for detection and imaging of \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) present in the real water samples and living cells. This study may inspire future research and design of target fluorescent MOFs with specific functions.
