## 2.5. Cell experiments
## 2.5.1. Cell culture
Human hepatoma cell \(({\mathrm{HepG}}_{2})\) line was grown in complete growth medium (containing \(90\%\) RPMI-1640, \(10\%\) fetal calf serum, \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) penicillin, and \(100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) streptomycin). The cells were cultured in an atmosphere with \(95\%\) humidity at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under \(5\%\) carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\right)\) environment.
## 2.5.2. Cytotoxicity study
The cytotoxic effects of Zn-MOF and solvent DMF were evaluated by the methyl thiazolyl tetrazolium (MTT) assay. \(\mathrm{HepG}_{2}\) cells \((10\times10^{4}\) cells/well) were inoculated into 96-well culture plate for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) with varying concentration of Zn-MOF dispersion (2.0, 4.0, 8.0, 12.0, 20.0, and \(30.0\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) ), followed by cultivation at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) under \(5\%\ C O_{2}\) . Next, the cells were incubated with \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(5.0\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) MTT phosphate-buffered saline (PBS, \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\) ) solution. After cultivation at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) , the MTT solution was removed gently using micro syringe and replaced with DMSO \((150\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) . Then the 96-well culture plate was transferred to a micro vibration oscillator for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) . The absorbance value of each well was recorded using a micro-plate reader (Huake, Shanghai, China) at \(490\,\mathrm{nm}\) . The cell viability rate (VR) was calculated according to the following equation:
\[
\mathsf{VR}=\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{A}_0\times100\%
\]
where \(\mathsf{A}_{0}\) and A are the mean absorbance value of the control group and experimental group, respectively. The experiments were carried out in six sets for each concentration.
2.5.3. Fluorescence microscopy imaging of cadmium ions in HepG2 cells
The cells were seeded into a six-well plate in a culture media at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) under \(5\%\,{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) . When the cells were \({\sim}90\%\) confluent, the coverslips were washed three times with PBS \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\\) . Then four groups of HepG2 cells were treated and imaged, respectively. The first group of cells was the control group. The second group of cells was incubated with \(8.0~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Zn-MOF at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1.0\,\mathrm{h}\) . The third group of cells was treated with \(8.0~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Zn-MOF at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1.0\,\mathrm{h}\) , and then incubated with \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) \((50.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for \(1.0\,\mathrm{h}\) . The fourth group of cells was incubated with \(8.0\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) ZnMOF and \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) \((50.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for \(1.0\,\mathrm{h}\) , which was followed by treatment with \(100.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) EDTA. Finally, the control group and treated cells were rinsed three times with PBS to remove the unbounded species, and the imaging of the cells was observed under a fluorescence inverted microscope at \(450\,\mathrm{nm}\) excitation wavelength. The Zn-MOF emission was collected at \(515\,\mathrm{nm}\) . A \(60\times\) objective lens was used.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Description of crystal structures
The single crystal X-ray diffraction measurement revealed that Zn-MOF crystallized in the monoclinic \(P2_{1}/c\) space group (Table S1 in ESI). Fig. 1a exhibits the existence of two types of independent
The single crystal X-ray diffraction measurement revealed that Zn-MOF crystallized in the monoclinic \(P2_{1}/c\) space group (Table S1 in ESI). Fig. 1a exhibits the existence of two types of independent \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\) ions (Zn1 and Zn2, based on coordination), two ligands, two coordinated DMF molecules, and one coordinated water molecule in a Zn-MOF asymmetric unit. Fig. 1b demonstrates that after the symmetry operation, Zn1 is six-coordinated to form an octahedral geometry constructed by two carboxyl oxygen atoms from two different ligands (O1 and O3ii), two oxygen atoms from two different DMF molecules (O7 and O8), and two oxygen atoms from two different water molecules (O9 and O9i). However, Zn2 is typically four-coordinated with three independent carboxyl groups (O2, O5, and O4ii) and one oxygen atom from water molecule (O9) to form tetrahedral coordination geometry. The Zn-O bond lengths are in the range of \(1.936{-}2.150\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) (see Table S2 in ESI), which are similar to the values of other Zn compounds [45,46]. The two carboxyl groups of L adopt bidentate bridging mode to combine two Zn ions and generate a bimetallic unit. Two bimetallic units are further linked with two water molecules to form a four-metal cluster structure secondary building unit (SBU) \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{L})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}.\) - \(\mathrm{O})_{2}\) (Fig. 1c). Moreover, the SBU is connected with adjacent units by another distinct coordination mode ligand L and extended into a 3D framework structure (Fig. 1d).
Furthermore, the PXRD patterns of the simulated and as-synthesized Zn-MOF are in well agreement with each other (Fig. S6), thus validating the successful synthesis of the new ZnMOF.
FTIR spectra of Zn-MOF and ligand L were obtained and illustrated in Fig. S7. The absorption band appearing at \({\sim}3400\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is attributed to the \(\mathsf{v}_{(\mathsf{O}^{-}\mathsf{H})}\) vibration of coordinated and lattice water molecules. The characteristic \(\mathsf{v}_{(\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O})}\) absorption peak appears as strong band at \({\sim}1658\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . Compared to the free carboxyl groups of ligand L whose absorption band of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{O}\) is at \(1664\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , the blue shift of this band indicates the coordination between carboxyl groups and \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) [17]. This result is in accordance with the crystal structure data.
The thermal stability of Zn-MOF was examined by TGA. Fig. S8 reveals that Zn-MOF loses its adsorbed water molecules \((4.4\%)\) at the temperature in the range of \(25{-}115\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Then it releases its internal coordinated DMF and water molecules \((22.8\%)\) from 115 to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (calcd: \(20.2\%\) ). Above \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , Zn-MOF starts to collapse.
The morphology of the Zn-MOF was characterized by SEM and TEM. The SEM image (Fig. S9a) reveals the stacked multilayer rod-shaped morphology of the Zn-MOF. Furthermore, TEM image (Fig. S9b) displays the branched Zn-MOF crystal structure.
