## 3.3. Sensing of metal ions
The selectivity of the sensing ability for various metal ions was studied and performed by addition of chloride \( \left( \mathrm{Li}^{+} \text{ and } \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} \right) \) and nitrate salts of \( \mathrm{Na}^{+} \), \( \mathrm{K}^{+} \), \( \mathrm{Ca}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Mg}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Ba}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \), \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \), \( \mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Mn}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \), and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) to the Zn-MOF suspensions in
DMF, with the concentration of metal ions being \( 20.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M} \), respectively. Fig. 2a shows the enhancement in the fluorescence signal of Zn-MOF with the addition of \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) ions; however, other ions exhibit a negligible effect on the fluorescence intensity. At the same time, Fig. 2a also shows that the fluorescence emission of Zn-MOF undergoes a red-shift from 529 to \( 555\,\mathrm{nm} \) when \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) is introduced. However, the emission intensity of the Zn-MOF at \( 529\,\mathrm{nm} \) remains almost unchanged upon the addition of \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \), which allows the non-interfering detection of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \). Furthermore,
Fig. S13 shows the fluorescence responses of L to various metal ions. Among them, \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Mg}^{2+} \), and \( \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} \) enhance the fluorescence intensity of L at the original emission band and \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) and \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) ions induce slight enhancement in fluorescence with the red-shifted emission. These phenomena indicate that the \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) \( (\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}) \) and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) \( (\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Mg}^{2+} \), and \( \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} \) ) conjugate to L in different ways. Therefore, the intrusion of \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) into Zn-MOF can induce the chelation of \( \mathrm{Al}^{3+} \) to the ligand L of Zn-MOF, which provided a red-shifted emission. Correspondingly, upon addition of \( \mathrm{Mg}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \), and \( \mathrm{Hg}^{2+} \), the unchangeable fluorescence spectra of Zn-MOF (Fig. 2a) may be attributed to the weaker coordination capability of the ligand L with these ions than that with \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \), which restrains the ion exchange between \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) in Zn-MOF and these ions. In any case, these results indicate the high selectivity of Zn-MOF for the sensing and specific recognition of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) compared to the original ligand L.
## 3.3.1. Sensing of cadmium ions
In order to evaluate the fluorescence response time of Zn-MOF to \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \), the time-dependent fluorescence intensity of Zn-MOF for sensing of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) was measured. Fig. S14 reveals that the fluorescence responses of Zn-MOF to \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) increase with the incubation time and the fluorescence intensity reaches a plateau within 20 min. Further, the fluorescence intensity of Zn-MOF remains almost constant. Thus, 20 min was selected as the optimized time for detection of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \).
In view of the complex practical samples, the effect of the coexisting ions on Zn-MOF sensing of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) was also examined (Fig. S15). The results of these experiments revealed that the presence of coexisting ions \( \mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Mn}^{2+} \), \( \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} \), and \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) in the solution had slightly negative effect on the determination of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \).
ence of coexisting ions \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}\) , \({\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}\) , \({\mathrm{Ni}}^{2+}\) , and \(C0^{2+}\) in the solution had slightly negative effect on the determination of \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) .
Fig. 3 exhibits the fluorescence responses of Zn-MOF in the presence of different concentrations of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \). Quantitatively, the enhancing effect can be analyzed by using the Stern–Volmer equation as follows:
\[
\frac{I}{I_0}=1+K_{SV}[M]
\]
where \( \mathrm{I}_{0} \) and \( \mathrm{I} \) are the fluorescence intensity of the Zn-MOF suspension at \( 529\,\mathrm{nm} \) in the absence and presence of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \), respectively. \( \mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{SV}} \) is the enhancing constant and \( \mathrm{[M]} \) is the concentration of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \). As a result, a good linear relationship \( (\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9938) \) was obtained between \( \mathrm{I}/\mathrm{I}_{0} \) and \( [\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}] \) as presented in the inset of Fig. 3, indicating that the enhancing effect of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) on fluorescence of Zn-MOF agrees well with the Stern–Volmer mode. The \( \mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{SV}} \) value was calculated to be \( 2.42\times10^{5}\,\mathrm{M}^{-1} \), which is two orders of magnitude higher than the previously reported value of the CPM-17-Zn-Eu [22], and close to the value of UiO-bpydc [25]. Furthermore, the detection limit of Zn-MOF was estimated to be \( 0.12\,\upmu\mathrm{M} \) based on a signal-to-noise ratio \( (S/\mathrm{N}) \) of 3:1 [13,23]. At the same time, \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \)-induced yellow-color fluorescence could be clearly seen under UV light irradiation at \( 365\,\mathrm{nm} \) (the inset of Fig. 3). The as-synthesized Zn-MOF sensor exhibited a low detection limit for \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) compared to the other developed MOF sensors for \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) (Table 1) such as the reported Eu(III) functionalized Zr-based MOF [26]. Moreover, the detection limit is similar to that of the Zr-based (UiO-66) sensor [20]; however, higher than the lanthanide-based MOF sensor [23,25].
3.3.2. The mechanistic study on zinc-based metal organic framework for sensing cadmium ions
As mentioned above, the fluorescence signal of the ligand L interacting with \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) far surpasses that with \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) (Fig. S13). Simultaneously, in view of the similar outer shell electronic structures of \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \), ion-exchange interaction between \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) can be proposed as the possible mechanism responsible for fluorescence enhancement induced by \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \).
To verify this hypothesis, AAS was employed to analyze the content of \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) in the solid MOF before (Zn-MOF) and after (Zn-MOF/Cd) treatment with aqueous solution of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) \( (0.32\,\mathrm{mM}) \). The results (Table S3) show that the reduced percentage \( (-0.31\%) \) of \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) in Zn-MOF/Cd is almost similar to the increased percentage \( (+0.33\%) \) of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) in Zn-MOF/Cd. At the same time, the reduced amount of Zn in Zn-MOF/Cd could also be corroborated that had entered the supernatant. Moreover, the EDX and XPS patterns also confirmed that \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) existed in the Zn-MOF/Cd (Figs. S16 and S17a). These data reveal that \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) in Zn-MOF could be replaced by \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) when Zn-MOF was incubated with \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \). Furthermore, Fig. S17b shows that the \( \mathrm{O}\,1\,\mathrm{s} \) peak for carboxylic oxygen atoms at \( 529.8\,\mathrm{eV} \) in Zn-MOF gets shifted to \( 529.4\,\mathrm{eV} \) with the addition of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \), thus illuminating the coordination of \( \mathrm{Cd}^{2+} \) to the carboxyl group in Zn-MOF/Cd.
Furthermore, PXRD patterns (Fig. S18) reveal that the sample obtained by immersing Zn-MOF in DMF containing \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ultrapure water is significantly different from the original Zn-MOF, clearly indicating that the ultrapure water can lead to the collapse or change in the Zn-MOF framework. Such collapse of the crystal structure facilitates the efficiency of ion exchange between \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\). The above mentioned results (Figs. S13 and S17b) indicate that the fluorescence enhancement induced by \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) ions can be ascribed to ion-exchange between \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\). This can be attributed to the fact that the introduction of \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) into Zn-MOF may be prone to the formation of a more stable framework structure, i.e., Zn-Cd-MOF (Fig. S19), capable of promoting the rigidity of the entire system, restricting the rotation of \(\mathrm{L}\) in MOF, and thus reducing various non-radiative relaxations [23]. Therefore, \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) can enhance the fluorescence of the Zn-MOF.
## 3.3.3. Determination of cadmium ions in real water samples
In order to evaluate the analytical performance of the sensor, the real samples including tap water and yellow river and lake water spiked with different concentrations of \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) were, respectively, analyzed by the proposed approach. Table 2 reveals good recoveries between 91.2 and \(101.7\%\), which indicates that ZnMOF can be successfully applied for the quantitative determination of \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) in water samples.
## 3.3.4. Fluorescence imaging of cadmium ions in living cells
To explore whether Zn-MOF can be used for cell imaging, its toxicity to living cells was investigated by using MTT assay. Fig. S20 exhibits that the cellular viability of \(\mathrm{HepG}_{2}\) remains over \(94\%\) in the presence of \(4.0\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) Zn-MOF. It was verified that the cytotoxicity originated from solvent DMF. Fig. S20 also exhibits that more than \(88\%\) cells survive at \(8.0\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) of Zn-MOF. Based on the consideration of the toxicity and the fluorescence signal, we selected \(8.0\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) of Zn-MOF for intracellular \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) imaging experiments. Next, the selective fluorescence sensing ability of Zn-MOF toward \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) in cells was evaluated. Fig. 4 demonstrates that when living HepG2 cells were incubated with Zn-MOF, an obvious yellow fluorescence (Fig. 4c) was observed in contrast with just \(\mathrm{HepG}_{2}\) cells without Zn-MOF (Fig. 4a). However, when cells were preincubated with Zn-MOF and subsequently incubated with \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) \((50.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\), the fluorescence intensity enhanced significantly (Fig. 4e), thus validating the response of Zn-MOF to \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) in in vitro test. Furthermore, after the cells were incubated with ZnMOF and \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\), \(100.0\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) EDTA was added and the cells were treated for \(1.0\,\mathrm{h}\). Fig. \(4\,\mathrm{g}\) exhibits a decrease in the fluorescence intensity and the result is similar to that shown in Fig. 4c. This further demonstrates that the fluorescence enhancement of Zn-MOF originates from \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\). These results clearly indicate that Zn-MOF can be effectively used to monitor \(C\mathsf{d}^{2+}\) in living cells.
## 3.4. Sensing of small organic molecules
In order to further explore the sensing capability of Zn-MOF, the fluorescence responses of Zn-MOF to various organic solvents were investigated by addition of NB, DMF, aniline, NMP, acetonitrile, DMSO, DMA, PE, EtOH, phenol, THF, methanol, \(\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}\) , benzene, acetone, EA, and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to the Zn-MOF suspension in DMF. Fig. 5 displays the fluorescence responses of Zn-MOF for these organic solvents within \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. S21). Compared to other organic molecules, Zn-MOF shows clear quenching effect for NB. Moreover, Fig. S22 also shows that other organic solvents nearly have no influence on detection of NB. Subsequently, we carried out titration experiments to research the quenching effect of NB to Zn-MOF. Fig. 6 shows that the fluorescence intensity of the Zn-MOF suspension gradually decreases with an increased concentration of NB (15, 30, 60, 90, 120, 180, 240, and \(360\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) ), which can be attributed to NB sensor. The detection limit of Zn-MOF for NB is \(1.19\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (based on S/N of 3:1). The quenching efficiency was up to \(99.0\%\) at \(1325\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) (quenching percentage \(=(\mathrm{I}_{0}-\mathrm{I})/\mathrm{I}_{0}\) \(\times\,100\%\) , where \(\mathrm{I}_{0}\) and I are the fluorescence intensity of Zn-MOF suspension before and after exposure to the NB, respectively). This value is higher than most of the reported literature values (Table 3) [33–39].
The possible quenching mechanism of Zn-MOF toward NB was investigated. Owing to the highly electron deficient property of NB and the porosity of framework, the quenching mechanism might be ascribed to the electron transfer from the electron donor Zn-MOF to NB adsorbed on the Zn-MOF. To confirm this speculation, the theoretical computation was employed based on density functional theory with \(\mathrm{[B_{3}L Y P/6-31G\,(d,p)/}\) level by using Gauss 09 software [32,45]. Fig. 7 shows that the higher lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy of L \((-2.6741\;\mathrm{eV})\) than that of electron-deficient NB \((-2.9214\,\mathrm{eV})\) makes the electron transfer from LUMO orbit of Zn-MOF to the LUMO orbit of NB easier, which caused the fluorescence quenching phenomenon by photoinduced electron transfer.
## 4. Conclusion
A novel three-dimensional Zinc-based metal-organic framework (Zn-MOF), i.e., \(\{\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{C})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\) based on a carboxyl group ligand was successfully synthesized under solvothermal condition. The prepared Zn-MOF could selectively sense cadmium ion \((\mathbf{C}\mathbf{d}^{2+})\) through the fluorescence enhancement based on ion exchange mechanism. Moreover, the corresponding results confirmed that the proposed Zn-MOF can be used as practical sensor for detecting and imaging analysis of \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) in real water samples and living cells, respectively. Furthermore, the Zn-MOF also displayed a highly selective fluorescence quenching for the electron-withdrawing nitrobenzene (NB) based on the photoinduced electron transfer mechanism. The results in this study show that the sensing performance of the proposed Zn-MOF for detecting \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) and NB is superior to that of the most of the reported methods. It provides a new insight into the rational design of MOF-based probe for sensing the target analytes as follows: (i) For the fluorescence enhancement in MOF-based probes, the selected ligand should have the extended \(\pi\) -conjugation structure for energy adsorption and it should be capable of gaining an amplified fluorescence signal for the target analyte. At the same time, the fabricated MOF-based sensor should have available interaction sites for binding with the target analyte. (ii) The theoretical computation can be employed to select or design the available ligand for MOF for sensing of organic molecules. This design concept can be utilized to develop the desired MOF-based sensors in the future study.
